[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Commands, Parameters, and Event-to-Command Behaviours in Xamarin"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Xamarin.Forms, the Model-View-ViewModel (MVVM) pattern relies heavily on data binding. While properties bind data to the UI, user interactions like button clicks traditionally rely on code-behind event handlers. To keep our applications decoupled and testable, we use Commands, Command Parameters, and Event-to-Command Behaviours."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding ICommand"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The ICommand interface defines how user actions are handled in the ViewModel. It consists of three members: Execute (the action to perform), CanExecute (a method returning a boolean that determines if the action is currently allowed), and CanExecuteChanged (an event that signals when CanExecute needs to be re-evaluated)."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"using System.Windows.Input;

public class MyViewModel
{
    public ICommand SubmitCommand { get; }

    public MyViewModel()
    {
        SubmitCommand = new Command(OnSubmit, ValidateSubmit);
    }

    private bool ValidateSubmit()
    {
        return !string.IsNullOrEmpty(Username);
    }

    private void OnSubmit()
    {
        // Handle submission logic
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Passing Command Parameters"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Often, you need to pass data from the UI to your ViewModel when a command is executed. Xamarin controls that support commands usually provide a CommandParameter property for this exact purpose."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"<Button Text="Delete Item" 
        Command="{Binding DeleteItemCommand}" 
        CommandParameter="{Binding ItemId}" />"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling Non-Command Events with Behaviours"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Not all Xamarin.Forms controls expose a Command property (for instance, a ListView ItemTapped event or a SearchBar TextChanged event). To bind these events to ViewModel commands without writing event handling logic in the code-behind, we use EventToCommandBehavior implementations, often found in libraries like Prism or Xamarin.CommunityToolkit."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Encapsulates UI event handling into reusable components."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keeps the code-behind file clean and strictly adhering to MVVM."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Allows passing event arguments directly to the ViewModel via EventArgsConverter."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"<ListView ItemsSource="{Binding Items}">
    <ListView.Behaviors>
        <xct:EventToCommandBehavior 
            EventName="ItemTapped"
            Command="{Binding ItemTappedCommand}"
            EventArgsConverter="{StaticResource ItemTappedEventArgsConverter}" />
    </ListView.Behaviors>
</ListView>"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"By leveraging ICommand, CommandParameters, and Event-to-Command Behaviours, you ensure your Xamarin applications remain modular, highly testable, and maintainable."}]
